/-!
# Vanishing of first Čech cohomology and sections of abelian sheaves

Let `0 → F₁ → F₂ → F₃ → 0` be a short exact sequence of abelian sheaves on a site `C` and
let `U` be an object of `C`. Stacks 03F8 asserts that if `U` admits a cofinal system of
coverings `{U_i → U}` on which the first Čech cohomology group of `F₁` vanishes, then
`F₂(U) → F₃(U)` is surjective.

The proof is the one in the source. A section `s` of `F₃` over `U` lifts locally, so by
cofinality it lifts along some covering `{U_i → U}` of the chosen system: there are sections
`x i` of `F₂` over `U_i` mapping to `s`. On a fibre product `U_i ×_U U_j` the difference of
the two restrictions of `x` dies in `F₃`, hence comes from a unique section of `F₁`; these
sections assemble into a degree-one Čech cochain `c` for `F₁`, and `c` is a cocycle because
its image in the Čech complex of `F₂` is the coboundary of `x` and the Čech cochain map is
injective in each degree. Vanishing of `Ȟ¹({U_i → U}, F₁)` makes `c` a coboundary, say of a
family `τ`, and subtracting the image of `τ` from `x` produces sections of `F₂` over the
`U_i` which agree on the fibre products. The sheaf condition for `F₂` glues them to a
section over `U`, and the sheaf condition for `F₃` identifies its image with `s`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.CechSite.surjective_on_sections_of_isZero_cechCohomology`: vanishing of first
  Čech cohomology on a cofinal system of coverings implies surjectivity on sections.

## Implementation notes

A covering family is a `PreZeroHypercover`, as in Stacks 03AM; it is a covering when its
generated sieve belongs to the Grothendieck topology, and cofinality says that one of the
chosen families generates a sieve contained in any given covering sieve. The existence of
the finite fibre products used in the Čech complex is recorded explicitly rather than
assumed of the whole slice category.

Two pieces of bookkeeping take up most of the file. First, an epimorphism of abelian
sheaves is locally surjective on sections: the subpresheaf of `F₃` generated by the
sheafified image of `F₂` is itself a sheaf, so it is a subobject through which the
epimorphism factors, hence all of `F₃`. Second, the degree-`n` term of the Čech complex is
a product indexed by `(n+1)`-tuples, and sections of it are handled through the additive
equivalence `Stacks.CechSite.productAddEquiv` with the dependent function type; the
definitions standing between that product and the Čech complex are made reducible locally
so that the two spellings are interchangeable for `rw` and `simp`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03F8](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03F8)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03AM](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03AM)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

